  "note": "First election of the whole Londonderry Corporation (40 members) after proportional representation was abolished, on the restored wards; nominations closed Friday 5 January 1923 (polling day would have been 15 January). The Nationalists boycotted the election and no candidates were nominated for the South and West wards; the Returning Officer gave notice that the retiring aldermen and councillors for those wards would be deemed re-elected (Derry Journal, 8 and 10 January 1923), so no South or West contest is recorded here. North, East and Waterside were all filled unopposed after W. G. Webb, J.P., an official Unionist nominee for Waterside, withdrew: 22 official Unionists, one unofficial Unionist and one British Legion member according to the News-Letter (8 January 1923). The reports do not say which of each ward's members were aldermen. Mark and Babington were printed as aldermen, the others as councillors. Returned unopposed."
